From dd8fd4235d37d1178bfd7c3b33a34dad675736b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Paprocki <hi@rpaprocki.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:17:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 17/29] builds: a Recent builds page and expiring share links

Once you left a build's result page there was no way back to it, so a
build that should not go on a public download page (a client with a
password inside) could not be fetched again.

/builds (linked from the form and the result page) lists the builds of
the last RDGEN_RETENTION_DAYS with their files, status and console
hand-off. For a finished build it creates a share link:
/share/<token> shows the build's files and /share/<token>/<file>
downloads one, without a login. The token is 32 random bytes
(token_urlsafe), a link expires after 1, 7 or 30 days and never after
the build itself, it can be deleted at once, and it counts downloads.
Unknown, expired and deleted links all answer the same 404 page; the
share pages are no-store, noindex and no-referrer, and only GET/HEAD.

RDGEN_SHARE_BASE (e.g. https://console.example.com/share) makes the
links public: a reverse proxy there passes exactly the two share paths
to rdgen. Without it, links point at rdgen itself (LAN only).

The file checks of /download move into stored_file(), shared by both.
The poller drops expired links when it prunes; a pruned build takes its
links along.

